<H2>3&nbsp;&nbsp; Decomposition of Hironaka</H2><!--SEC END -->
<A NAME="Hironaka"></A>The smallest degree bound &beta;(<FONT COLOR=red> <I>I</I></FONT><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I></FONT></SUB>) and furthermore the polynomial
s(<FONT COLOR=red> <I>I</I></FONT><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I></FONT></SUB>,<I>z</I>) contains important information about the invariant
ring, which would be very useful when the computation of a minimal
generating set is intractable. But so far, we don't know how to
calculate them except by explicitly computing such a minimal
generating set.<BR>
<BR>
Invariant theory provides only some bounds on &beta;(<FONT COLOR=red> <I>I</I></FONT><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I></FONT></SUB>) and
s(<FONT COLOR=red> <I>I</I></FONT><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I></FONT></SUB>,<I>z</I>)&nbsp;[<A HREF="#Schmid.1991"><CITE>25</CITE></A><CITE>, </CITE><A HREF="#Derksen_Kraft.1997"><CITE>6</CITE></A>]. Noether's
theorem&nbsp;[<A HREF="#Sturmfels.AIT"><CITE>29</CITE></A><CITE>, p.&nbsp;27</CITE>] yields: &beta;(<FONT COLOR=red> <I>I</I></FONT><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I></FONT></SUB>)&le; |<FONT COLOR=red> <I>G</I></FONT><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I></FONT></SUB>|=<I>n</I>!,
which is not very informative. A much better bound exists for
permutation groups: &beta;(<FONT COLOR=red> <I>I</I></FONT><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I></FONT></SUB>)&le; (<TABLE CELLSPACING=2 CELLPADDING=0>
