</TR></TABLE>)&nbsp;[<A HREF="#Garsia_Stanton.1984"><CITE>11</CITE></A>]. However, our computations for
small <I>n</I> shows that this is still a rather loose bound. This section
introduces the tools that produce this bound, and possibly even better
bounds.<BR>
<BR>
A set of <I>m</I> homogeneous invariants (&theta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2>1</FONT></SUB>,...,&theta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>m</I></FONT></SUB>) of
<I>I</I>(<I>G</I>) is called a <EM>homogeneous system of parameters</EM> or, for
short, a <EM>system of parameters</EM> if the invariant ring <I>I</I>(<I>G</I>) is
finitely generated over its subring <I>K</I>[&theta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2>1</FONT></SUB>,...,&theta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>m</I></FONT></SUB>].
That is, if there exist a finite number of invariants
(&eta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2>1</FONT></SUB>,...,&eta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>t</I></FONT></SUB>) such that the invariant ring is the sum of the
subspaces &eta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>i</I></FONT></SUB>.<I>K</I>[&theta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2>1</FONT></SUB>,...,&theta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>m</I></FONT></SUB>]. By Noether's
normalization lemma, there always exists a system of parameters for
<I>I</I>(<I>G</I>). Moreover, <I>I</I>(<I>G</I>) is <EM>Cohen-Macaulay</EM>, which means that
<I>I</I>(<I>G</I>) is a free-module over any system of parameters. So, if the set
(&eta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2>1</FONT></SUB>,...,&eta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>t</I></FONT></SUB>) is minimal for inclusion, <I>I</I>(<I>G</I>) decomposes
into a direct sum:
<DIV ALIGN=center>
 <I>I</I>(<I>G</I>)=<FONT SIZE=7>&oplus;</FONT><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>i</I>=1</FONT></SUB><SUP><FONT SIZE=2><I>t</I></FONT></SUP> &eta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>i</I></FONT></SUB> . <I>K</I>[&theta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2>1</FONT></SUB>,...,&theta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>m</I></FONT></SUB>].
</DIV>
This decomposition is called a <EM>Hironaka decomposition</EM> of the
invariant ring. The &theta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>i</I></FONT></SUB> are called <EM>primary invariants</EM>,
and the &eta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>i</I></FONT></SUB> <EM>secondary invariants</EM> (in algebraic
combinatorics literature, the &theta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>i</I></FONT></SUB> are some times called
<EM>quasi-generators</EM> and the &eta;<SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>i</I></FONT></SUB>
<EM>separators</EM>&nbsp;[<A HREF="#Garsia_Stanton.1984"><CITE>11</CITE></A>]). It should be emphasized
that primary and secondary invariants are not uniquely determined, and
that being a primary or secondary invariant is not an intrinsic
property of an invariant <I>p</I>, but rather express the role of <I>p</I> in a
particular generating set.<BR>
<BR>
The primary and secondary invariants together form a generating
set. From the degrees (<I>d</I><SUB><FONT SIZE=2>1</FONT></SUB>,...,<I>d</I><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>m</I></FONT></SUB>) of the primary invariants
