 </EM><EM>SL</EM><EM>(<I>V</I>)</EM><EM>.<BR>
<BR>
 (iii) If </EM><EM><I>n</I></EM><EM> is odd, the representation of </EM><EM> <I>S</I></EM><SUB><EM><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I></FONT></EM></SUB><EM> on the
 irreducible component </EM><EM>[<I>n</I>-2,2]</EM><EM> is a subgroup of </EM><EM>SL</EM><EM>(<I>V</I>)</EM><EM>.
</EM></DIV>
<B>Proof</B>: 
 (i) If &sigma; is a transposition of 2 vertices, then
 &sigma; exchanges <I>n</I>-2 pairs of edges, and
 sign(&sigma;)=(-1)<SUP><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I>-2</FONT></SUP>.<BR>
<BR>
 (iii) Take &sigma;&isin; <I>S</I><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I></FONT></SUB>, and <I>M</I> the matrix of the
 representation of &sigma; on the component [<I>n</I>-2,2]. The
 determinant of the representation of &sigma; on <FONT COLOR=red> <I>V</I></FONT><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I></FONT></SUB> is
 sign(&sigma;), whereas the sign of the representation of
 &sigma; on the other component [<I>n</I>]&oplus;[<I>n</I>-1,1] is
 sign(&sigma;) (natural representation of  <I>S</I><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I></FONT></SUB>). Therefore,
 det(<I>M</I>)=sign(&sigma;)/sign(&sigma;)=1, if <I>n</I> is odd.
QED<BR>
<BR>
Then, Watanabee's theorem&nbsp;[<A HREF="#Stanley.1979"><CITE>27</CITE></A><CITE>, §&nbsp;8</CITE>] applies.
<DIV ALIGN=left><B>Theorem&nbsp;6.2</B>&nbsp;&nbsp;<EM>
 (i) When </EM><EM><I>n</I></EM><EM> is even, </EM><EM><FONT COLOR=red> <I>I</I></FONT></EM><SUB><EM><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I></FONT></EM></SUB><EM> is Gorenstein.<BR>
<BR>
 (ii) When </EM><EM><I>n</I></EM><EM> is odd, the invariant ring over the irreducible
 component </EM><EM>[<I>n</I>-2,2]</EM><EM> is Gorenstein.
</EM></DIV><BR>
