<BR>
This was disappointing. Indeed, the invariant ring <FONT COLOR=red> <I>I</I></FONT><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I></FONT></SUB> is the
quotient ring of <FONT COLOR=red> <I>I</I></FONT><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>n</I></FONT></SUB> by the ideal generated by <I>x</I><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>i</I>,<I>i</I></FONT></SUB>=0 and
<I>x</I><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>i</I>,<I>j</I></FONT></SUB>=<I>x</I><SUB><FONT SIZE=2><I>j</I>,<I>i</I></FONT></SUB>. Therefore, we could have used lemma&nbsp;1 to prove that
